# Byte-exact storage. Do not change these lines without reading the reasoning.
#
# `-text` tells git this path has NO text semantics: never normalise line endings
# on commit, never convert them on checkout. Files come back out of git exactly as
# they went in, byte for byte.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS HERE, MORE THAN IN AN ORDINARY REPO
#
# git is this project's rollback target (NIGHTLY_DESIGN.md §6), and three separate
# mechanisms compare these files by exact bytes and sha256:
#
#   * coordinator/circle_close_verify.py  writes each short_term's sha256 into
#     work/logs/close_<OT>.json at close, and --reconcile re-verifies it before
#     dreaming. A checkout that "helpfully" rewrote LF to CRLF would report
#     RECONCILE-DRIFT on files nobody had touched -- and the documented response to
#     DRIFT is to backfill from the transcript, i.e. to overwrite a good record.
#
#   * coordinator/record_model.py  enforces that dream-entry bodies, the identity
#     preamble, and unchanged sections are byte-identical. Line-ending conversion
#     would fail every one of those checks at once.
#
#   * work/nightly/<RUN>/rollback/  restores by copying bytes back over the live
#     paths. A rollback that changes the file it restores is not a rollback.
#
# The tree is also legitimately mixed: coordinator/circle.py wrote CRLF on Windows
# (fixed 2026-07-26, but the 2026-07-26_1910 circle's eight files remain CRLF and
# their sha256s are already recorded in work/logs/close_2026-07-26_1910.json).
# `-text` preserves that history instead of rewriting it.
#
# Also set, locally:  git config core.autocrlf false

* -text
